    "reading": {
        "body": "Minnesota's mapped data center footprint totals 9 MW of recorded capacity across 13 facilities, a figure so low it reflects incomplete disclosure rather than the state's true infrastructure scale.\n\nOnly one facility, Flexential Minneapolis–Chaska, carries a power figure in this dataset, and that single site accounts for all 9 MW attributed to the state. The remaining 12 facilities report no capacity at all. Geographic concentration is visible in Chaska, which holds three facilities within coordinates close enough to suggest a shared industrial corridor, while a second cluster sits in the Minneapolis–Saint Paul metro. One facility, the Epic/Mayo Data Center in Rochester, suggests healthcare-driven compute demand separate from the commercial colocation market.\n\nFor AI workloads, raw megawatt totals are the operative constraint, and this dataset cannot support any conclusion about Minnesota's ability to host them. The missing capacity figures mean the 9 MW total should be read as a floor, not a ceiling. What the data does establish is the shape of the ecosystem: a mix of commercial colocation operators, a carrier presence through Lumen and Verizon, a university research facility, and at least one purpose-built healthcare anchor, covering a geography that stretches from the Twin Cities metro north to Duluth.",
